<p>The US military said it carried out an airstrike on an alleged drug-smuggling vessel in the eastern Pacific on Friday, killing three people, according to statements from US Southern Command and the Pentagon. US Southern Command said intelligence indicated the boat was moving along “known narco-trafficking routes” and was involved in drug trafficking. The strike is the second reported US attack on suspected smuggling boats this week, and the sixth known strike this year. That brings the reported death toll from these operations to at least 148 people.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/U8PqUGpQ7s7B2z8Wwdi4ie2wDtWzPBG8Tt7YsdBYVOyltxKRJZ2oI16MLZcaXA4PF4b_6koF56A-UcaEXKEvPtNuCD_figR3watSAGRFqgtt48lpqihUVlr7j3vddcsmRFUoUU3IyuWN72ftnklGwYuKsV1nxKzqQYF9zvuKB_k93bGgAcXgkNaeATIuYqsnxVmKoWmbWO49P-fT6-c-OtWqFw' alt='A handout-style image used with reporting on El Salvador’s maritime drug seizures.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>This week’s toll: PBS reported that US boat strikes this week left 11 people dead across three boats suspected of carrying drugs in Latin American waters.</li><li>How it was framed: The South China Morning Post described the targeted craft as a “narcoterrorist” boat.</li><li>Video released: SOUTHCOM posted video it said shows a boat burning after the strike.</li></ul><blockquote>Intelligence confirmed the vessel was transiting along known narco-trafficking routes in the Eastern Pacific and was engaged in narco-trafficking operations. - US Southern Command</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>US Southern Command (SOUTHCOM): SOUTHCOM said intelligence confirmed the vessel was traveling along known trafficking routes and was involved in narco-trafficking operations. It also publicized the strike on social media and posted video. (<a href='https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/feb/20/us-military-strike-drug-smuggling-pacific'>The Guardian</a>)</li><li>Al Jazeera on the US claims: Al Jazeera noted that SOUTHCOM did not provide evidence for its claim that the vessel was involved in drug trafficking. (<a href='https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/2/21/us-military-says-it-attacked-vessel-in-pacific-ocean-killing-three-people'>Al Jazeera</a>)</li><li>Mexico and El Salvador navies (via PBS): PBS highlighted that Mexico’s and El Salvador’s navies announced Pacific seizures totaling more than 10 tonnes of cocaine this week, contrasting those results with the ongoing lethal US boat strikes. (<a href='https://www.pbs.org/newshour/world/mexico-and-el-salvador-make-big-cocaine-seizures-at-sea-as-lethal-u-s-boat-strikes-continue'>PBS NewsHour</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/feb/20/us-military-strike-drug-smuggling-pacific'>US military strike kills three in second alleged drug boat attack this week</a> - theguardian.com</li><li><a href='https://www.dw.com/en/us-strike-kills-three-on-boat-in-eastern-pacific/a-76068561'>US strike kills three on boat in eastern Pacific</a> - dw.com</li><li><a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/us-says-it-struck-vessel-in-the-eastern-pacific-killing-three-men'>Three dead in latest US strike on alleged drug boat: Pentagon</a> - straitstimes.com</li><li><a href='https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/2/21/us-military-says-it-attacked-vessel-in-pacific-ocean-killing-three-people'>US military says it attacked vessel in Pacific Ocean, killing three people</a> - aljazeera.com</li><li><a href='https://www.pbs.org/newshour/world/mexico-and-el-salvador-make-big-cocaine-seizures-at-sea-as-lethal-u-s-boat-strikes-continue'>Mexico and El Salvador make big cocaine seizures at sea as lethal U.S. boat strikes continue</a> - pbs.org</li><li><a href='https://www.scmp.com/news/world/united-states-canada/article/3344191/us-military-kills-3-latest-strike-narcoterrorist-boat'>US military kills 3 in latest strike on ‘narcoterrorist’ boat</a> - scmp.com</li><li><a href='https://www.theglobeandmail.com/world/article-us-military-strikes-trump-alleged-drug-boat/'>U.S. strikes alleged drug-smuggling boat in eastern Pacific, killing three people</a> - theglobeandmail.com</li></ul>